  "question": {
    "text": "Two point masses of mass $m_1=f M$ and $m_2=(1-f) M(f < 1)$ are in outer space (far from gravitational influence of other objects) at a distance $R$ from each other. They move in circular orbits about their centre of mass with angular velocities $\\omega_1$ for $m_1$ and $\\omega_2$ for $m_2$. In that case",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "Sgl35",
      "text": "\n$(1-f) \\omega_1=f \\omega$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "HZo9F",
      "text": "\n$\\omega_1=\\omega_2$ and independent of $f$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "06hqj",
      "text": "\n$f \\omega_1=(1-f) \\omega_2$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "NyVIx",
      "text": "\n$\\omega_1=\\omega_2$ and depend on $f$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Angular velocity is the angular displacement per unit time i.e., $\\omega=\\frac{\\Delta \\theta}{\\Delta t}$ Here $w_1=w_2$ and independent of $f$.",
    "image": null
  },
